The six-page <University of Oxford> Finals <computer graphics> paper dated Monday 8 June 2015. The released https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/computer_science_past_exam_paper/response/956037/attach/3/Lawrence%20Attachment.zip?cookie_passthrough=1[examination archive] contains it as `2015-graphics.pdf`. Questions occupy printed pp. 2–6. Question 2(b)(iii)'s two printed rotation products require an action-order qualification: their intended comparison works when the rotations are applied in written sequence, while ordinary column-vector products apply the rightmost factor first.

= Understanding POWER Multiprocessors (2011)
{parent=Computer science bibliography}
{c}
{title2=PLDI 2011}

Primary research article: https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~pes20/ppc-supplemental/pldi105-sarkar.pdf

Section 2.1 introduces store buffering and message passing. Section 2.2 explains barriers and dependencies, including why a genuine <address dependency> can order reads while a bare <control dependency> does not. This source corroborates the general <memory model> discussion, not the availability of examination answers.

