= Scala 2.11 basic declarations and definitions specification
{c}
{parent=Computer science bibliography}

https://www.scala-lang.org/files/archive/spec/2.11/04-basic-declarations-and-definitions.html

The “Variance Annotations” section specifies variance positions and lower-bound widening, used when an immutable class's output <type parameter> is covariant while a method must accept a possibly broader output value.

= Discrete mathematics, continuous mathematics, and probability examination (2015) by University of Oxford
{parent=Computer science bibliography}
{title2=A10100W1}
{title2=2015-06-25}

The <University of Oxford> preliminary <computer science> examination whose original cover gives Trinity Term 2015, Thursday 25 June, and formal code A10100W1. The released archive contains it as `2015-cs3.pdf`: https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/computer_science_past_exam_paper/response/956037/attach/3/Lawrence%20Attachment.zip?cookie_passthrough=1[publicly released examination archive]. Printed pages 2–4 contain Questions 1–3; pages 5–7 contain Questions 4–6; pages 8–10 contain Questions 7–9.

The printed <codomain> in Question 2(b) is inconsistent with its formula, and an unrestricted zero extension of the <binomial coefficients> makes the premise in (ii) vacuous beyond the finite row. Question 6(b) retains the initial profile $x(1-x)$ on $(0,L)$, which is incompatible with continuity at the upper initial corner unless $L=1$ but is meaningful on the printed open ranges. Question 9(c)(iii) omits the integration variable after both differential signs. These are source qualifications, not missing source material.

= Discrete mathematics, continuous mathematics, and probability resit examination by the University of Oxford (2015)
{parent=Computer science bibliography}
{title2=2015-09-09}
{title2=A10100W1}

Primary source: https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/past_exam_papers_for_1st_year_of/response/1398416/attach/2/Fakour%20Zip%20Final%201.zip?cookie_passthrough=1[released examination archive], member `a10100w1-4.pdf`. The original eleven-page cover identifies the Preliminary Examination in Computer Science, Long Vacation 2015, Wednesday 9 September. The archive folder name is not the examination year. Page 8, Question 6(b)(iii), explicitly identifies “saddle point” with “point of inflexion”; that usage is specific to this paper. The definition in Question 6(a)(ii) leaves endpoint values unspecified, which does not affect its <Fourier series>.

= Digital systems, linear algebra, introduction to formal proof examination paper by the University of Oxford (2015)
{parent=Computer science bibliography}
{title2=A10101W1}

Examination paper of the <University of Oxford>, Preliminary Examination in Computer Science, Trinity Term 2015, dated 19 June 2015. The original has seven pages; the last contains the prescribed <natural deduction> rules. Released archive: https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/computer_science_past_exam_paper/response/956037/attach/3/Lawrence%20Attachment.zip?cookie_passthrough=1[Lawrence Attachment.zip], member `2015-cs4.pdf`. The paper's printed section order is <linear algebra>, <digital electronics>, and introduction to <formal proof>, despite the different ordering in its cover title.

