= Thumb 16-bit instruction set quick reference by Arm
{parent=Computer science bibliography}
{c}

https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/5ed67dc7ca06a95ce53f9334

Page 1 lists the compact logical and shift instruction forms. <Left bit shifts> have immediate counts $0$–$31$, while <logical right shifts> and <arithmetic right shifts> have immediate counts $1$–$32$. The logical <bitwise AND> entry uses a <processor register> operand. These encoding details explain why extracting low <bits> with two immediate shifts can avoid materializing a <bit mask>.

= C11 committee draft N1570
{parent=Computer science bibliography}
{c}

https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg14/www/docs/n1570.pdf

The 12 April 2011 committee draft specifies <C integer division> in section 6.5.5, especially paragraphs 5–6 and footnote 105. It fixes the truncation and quotient–remainder relationship used when verifying <signed power-of-two division by biasing>.

= Lecture 15: Repeated Games by Asu Ozdaglar
{parent=Computer science bibliography}
{c}
{title2=2010}

Lecture 15 of MIT 6.254, Game Theory with Engineering Applications, 1 April 2010, by <Asu Ozdaglar>. Slides 19–20 define feasible and minmax payoffs; slide 24 states the discounted Nash folk theorem; slides 25–27 explain punishment and credibility.

Official PDF: https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-254-game-theory-with-engineering-applications-spring-2010/5a158a7558165d0e22b5b27fcfa01713_MIT6_254S10_lec15.pdf

= Maximum-weight matching documentation by NetworkX
{parent=Computer science bibliography}

https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/algorithms/generated/networkx.algorithms.matching.max_weight_matching.html

The `max_weight_matching` documentation specifies unrestricted-weight optimization by default, distinguishes the optional maximum-<cardinality> constraint, and gives cubic running time in the number of <vertices>. Its notes distinguish exact integer arithmetic from possible precision effects for floating-point weights. These details specify the <maximum-weight matching> subroutine needed for <minimum-weight edge cover by matching savings>.

